{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father as the Count of Tripoli after his death in 1152.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\nRaymond III was born around 1140. His early life is not well documented but it is known that he was raised in the court of his father Raymond II. Raymond III succeeded his father at a young age and faced numerous challenges during his early reign including the threat of the Byzantine Empire and the Muslim states of Syria.\n\nRaymond III married Hodierna of Jerusalem's niece Eschiva of Bures in 1174. The marriage was likely arranged to strengthen the alliance between the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. The couple had two daughters but no sons which led to a succession crisis after Raymond III's death.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin*\nIn 1187 Raymond III joined the forces of King Guy of Lusignan of Jerusalem to confront the Ayyubid forces led by Saladin. The two armies clashed at the Battle of Hattin on July 4 1187. The Crusader army was defeated and many of its leaders including King Guy were captured. Raymond III escaped the battlefield but died on September 1 1187 while fleeing to Tripoli.\n\n*Legacy and Succession*\nRaymond III's death led to a succession crisis in the County of Tripoli. His nephew Raymond IV of Antioch claimed the throne but was opposed by Bohemond III of Antioch and the Templars. The succession crisis was eventually resolved with the Templars supporting the claim of Raymond IV. Raymond III's legacy is complex and has been debated among historians. Some view him as a weak and ineffective leader while others see him as a capable ruler who faced significant challenges during his reign.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004134069196879864}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token